// String literals, casts, sizeof.

// A string literal is an address, so it has to exist in the flat store.
// mem_of_str copies the text in and hands back the address, which is
// precisely C's semantics apart from the lifetime. The spelling is passed
// through unchanged, escapes and all -- the lexer kept the raw text between
// the quotes, and the two languages agree on the escapes that appear in
// practice. chr(34) rather than a quote character because writing one inside
// an `id` string would need an escape this program would then have to know.
str_txt(int id) {
  string s = "mem_of_str(" + chr(34) + s_of(id) + chr(34) + ")";
  string out = name_val(s);
} return string out;

// A cast is exactly the width conversion, which is the same operation an
// assignment or a load performs; casting to a pointer or to any 8-byte type
// is then nothing at all, because the value is already 64 bits wide.
cast_txt(int id) {
  int b = b_of(id);
  string exa = a_ex(id);
  string s = conv_txt(b, exa);
} return string s;

// sizeof is answered at translation time -- it is the one C operator that
// never survives into the output. nb holds the type node for `sizeof(T)`;
// otherwise the operand's type is inferred and measured.
sizeof_txt(int id) {
  int sz = sizeof_sz(id);
  string s = "" + sz;
} return string s;
